	PATCH.ALG IS AN ALGOL ORIENTED VERSION OF THE RT-11 COUNTERPART
PATCH.SAV. 'PATCH.ALG' (HEREAFTER REFERRED TO AS PATCH) ALLOWS
MODIFYCATIONS TO CODE FILES WITHIN CERTAIN RESTRICTIONS.

	1) ONLY THE PROGRAM REFERANCE TABLE (PRT) AND CODE SEGMENTS
	   MAY BE LISTED AND/OR MODIFIED. THIS RESTRICTION IS DUE TO
	   THE FACT THAT THE INFO ON DATA SEGMENTS IS STORED ONLY IN
	   THE PROGRAM SEGMENT WHICH LOADS THEM (VIA THE FILL STATEMENT)
	   AND NOT IN THE PRT.

	2) ANY CODE SEGMENT (OR THE PRT) MAY BE EXTENDED BEYOUND
	   ITS CURRENTLY ALLOCATED SPACE BUT MAY NOT BE EXTENDED
	   BEYOUND THE PHYSICAL BLOCK BOUNDRY IN WHICH THE LAST 255
	   (OR FEWER) WORDS LIE. IE. THE CODE FILE IS NOT MOVED AROUND
	   TO MAKE MORE ROOM. THIS WOULD BECOME A TIMELY PROCESS AS WELL
	   AS A COMPLICATED PROCESS (SAME REASON AS FOR RESTRICTION #1).

	3) IN GENERAL PATCHED '.ALG' FILES WILL NOT BE SUPPORTED.
	   IE. IF YOU SCREW UP, ITS YOUR PROBLEM. THE INTERPRETER
	   EXPECTS 'GOOD' CODE & DOES VERY LITTLE CHECKING OF ITS
	   GOODNESS. 'BAD CODE' WILL GENERALLY CRASH THE INTERPRETER
	   POSSIBLY BEYOUND THE POINT OF GETTING A STACK HISTORY,
	   AND POSSIBLY EVEN CRASHING THE MONITOR.

IT IS SUGGESTED THAT YOU WORK WITH A CURRENTLY COMPILED LISTING WITH
THE CODE AND STACK SWITCHES ON (@D, @P, SEE THESIS).

TO MODIFY A FILE:

.R ALGOL<CR>
 -----------
ALGOL INTERPRETER V06.6.001
CODE FILE: PATCH<CR>
	   ---------
FILE NAME - FOO<CR>	(DEFAULT EXTENSION OF '.ALG' IS ASSUMED)
	    -------

PATCH ASSUMES THAT IT IS MODIFYING AN HONEST TO GOSH ALGOL CODE FILE.
GIVE IT SOMETHING OTHER THAN WHAT IT EXPECTS AND ITS THE OLD SAME CASE
OF GIGO (GARBAGE-IN, GARBAGE-OUT).

PATCH REPLIES
*

AT THIS POINT THERE ARE THREE (3) ALTERNATIVES.

	1. EXIT

	    TO EXIT TYPE
		*E<CR>
		 -----

	2. LIST A SECTION OF CODE (OR THE PRT).

	    TO LIST A SECTION OF CODE SPECIFY
		*[SEGNO:]STARTPC[-STOPPC]<CR>
		 ----------------------------

	    SEGNO IS THE SEGMENT TO LIST. IF NOT SPECIFIED DEFAULTS TO
	    ZERO. SEGMENT 0 IS THE PRT. STARTPC IS THE INTERPRETIVE PC
	    TO START THE LISTING AT WITHIN THE SEGMENT. STOPPC IS WHERE
	    TO STOP. IF NOT SPECIFIED DEFAULTS TO THE STARTPC.

	    IE.
		*114<CR>
		 -------

		WILL LIST PRT[114] (THE 115TH ENTRY, 0 IS THE 1ST).

		*2:10-17<CR>
		 -----------

		WILL LIST RELATIVE PC'S 10 THROUGH 17 IN SEGMENT 2.


	3. EDIT MODE

	    TO GET INTO EDIT MODE SPECIFY ONE OF THE FOLLOWING.

		*[SEGNO:]PC/<CR>	(OPEN LOCATION AS WORD)
		 ---------------

			OR

		*[SEGNO:]PC\<CR>	(OPEN LOCATION AS BYTE)
		 ---------------

			OR

		*[SEGNO:]PC+1\<CR>	(OPEN UPPER BYTE OF LOCATION)
		 -----------------

	    THE '/' OPENS THE LOCATION AS A WORD, THE '\' OPENS THE
	    LOCATION AS A BYTE.

	    IF SEGNO IS ABSENT THEN SEG #0 IS ASSUMED (THE PRT).

	    ON THE LINE PRINTED, THE # AFTER THE '/' OR '\' IS THE
	    CONTENTS OF THE OPENED LOCATION. IF THAT LOCATION IS NOT
	    CURRENTLY IN THE SEGMENT THEN 6 SPACES WILL APPEAR.

	    PROPER RESPONSES ARE:
		/<CR>		GO TO WORD MODE.
		\<CR>		GO TO BYTE MODE.
		^<CR>		GO TO PREVIOUS LOCATION.
		<CR>		GO TO NEXT LOCATION.
		<OCTAL #><CR>	DEPOSIT <OCTAL #> IN LOCATION, GO TO NEXT
				LOCATION.
		*<CR>		EXIT EDIT MODE.
		E<CR>		EXIT PROGRAM.
